# MalloySim scenario: rigid bodies on true flat ground.
#
# This is what M16 adds. Every contact in this project was disc against disc,
# so a floor had to be built out of overlapping discs. scenarios/dropped_bodies.scn
# still does exactly that, and documents the result as a surface scalloped by
# about 0.05.
#
# That height ripple is the wrong number to worry about. What matters is the
# contact NORMAL, which on a disc floor points at whichever disc centre is
# nearest and therefore SWINGS as a body moves along it: by up to 14 degrees
# for the floor in dropped_bodies.scn. A body sliding across it gets steered
# and spun by a surface that is supposed to be flat.
#
# A halfplane has one normal, everywhere, forever. The contact against it is
# also the only pair in malloy_collide with no degenerate case: the plane
# supplies the direction, so nothing has to be inferred from the relative
# position of two centres, and none of the documented fallbacks apply.
#
# Three planes here, and they are infinite, which is the thing to keep in mind
# when reading the layout: the ramp does not stop where it looks like it should.
# The reachable region is wherever ALL of them are satisfied at once, so the
# ramp cuts off the left half of the floor entirely.

type rigid

dt 0.001
steps 5000
output_every 500

gravity 0.0 -9.81
restitution 0.35

# ground <nx> <ny> <offset>: the line dot((nx,ny), p) == offset, with the normal
# pointing OUT of the solid. The direction is normalized on load, so 3 4 is
# accepted and becomes the exact 3-4-5 unit normal (0.6, 0.8).

# The floor: y = -2.
ground 0 1 -2

# A ramp descending to the right, through the origin. Normal (0.6, 0.8) means
# an incline whose sine is exactly 0.6, so a frictionless body on it
# accelerates down the slope at exactly g * 0.6 = 5.886.
ground 3 4 0

# A wall at x = 6, keeping bodies to its left.
ground -1 0 -6

# rigid_body <mass> <inertia> <radius> <comx> <comy> <px> <py> <angle> <vx> <vy> <omega>

# Released from rest exactly touching the ramp: 0.6*(-2.0) + 0.8*(2.0) = 0.4,
# which is its radius. It slides, because nothing here has friction.
rigid_body 1.00  0.08  0.40   0.00  0.00   -2.00  2.00  0.00    0.00  0.00  0.00

# Dropped straight onto the flat floor, where the ramp no longer reaches.
rigid_body 1.50  0.20  0.50   0.00  0.00    4.50  5.00  0.00    0.00  0.00  0.00

# Expected, from a real run of the shipped binary:
#
#   step      0   E   9.31950000e+01   p   0.00000000e+00
#   step    500   E   9.31502523e+01   p   2.35562080e+00
#   step   1000   E   9.31055025e+01   p   4.71002080e+00
#   step   3000   E  -3.67974452e+01   p  -1.70679607e+00
#   step   3500   E  -3.67974452e+01   p  -1.70679607e+00
#   step   4000   E  -3.67974452e+01   p  -1.70679607e+00
#
# Three things in that table are checkable rather than merely observed.
#
# 1. The starting energy is all potential and sums by hand. With E measured
#    against y = 0, it is 9.81*1.0*2.0 + 9.81*1.5*5.0 = 93.195 exactly, which
#    is what the first line prints.
#
# 2. The ramp acceleration is exact. A frictionless body on an incline whose
#    sine is 0.6 accelerates down the slope at g*0.6 = 5.886, and the slope
#    direction is (0.8, -0.6), so the HORIZONTAL acceleration is
#    9.81 * 0.6 * 0.8 = 4.7088. Only the ramp body has horizontal motion while
#    the other is still falling, and its mass is 1, so the p column is its
#    velocity directly: (4.71002080 - 2.35562080) / 0.5 = 4.7088, agreeing with
#    the prediction to 9e-16. That number is the milestone: on a floor built
#    from discs there is no constant incline angle to predict it from.
#
# 3. Momentum is EXACTLY unchanged across steps 3000, 3500 and 4000, to every
#    printed digit. One body is sliding along the flat floor and nothing slows
#    it, because the contact normal never turns and there is no friction. On
#    the scalloped disc floor of dropped_bodies.scn the normal swings by up to
#    14 degrees, which would steer the body and change this column every frame.
#
# Point 3 is what M17 then changed. Friction is a setting, and this scenario
# leaves it at its default of zero, so a body that lands here still slides
# forever. See scenarios/rolling_and_slipping.scn for the same floor with
# friction turned on, where a disc settles into rolling instead.
#
# The big fall in E between steps 1000 and 1500 is the impacts: the ramp body
# reaches the floor and strikes the other one, and restitution 0.35 takes its
# cut at every bounce. E is not conserved here and is not meant to be: the
# planes are immovable, so they are external to the system, and the final
# change at step 4500 is the wall.
#
# The planes themselves do not appear in the ASCII view, which plots bodies.
# The view is better for it: it fits the bodies, where a floor built from discs
# drags the viewport down to the floor discs' centres.
